A \(2 \times 2\) ROM array is built with the help of diodes as shown in the circuit below. Here \(W0\) and \(W1\) are signals that select the word lines and \(B0\) and \(B1\) are signals that are output of the sense amps based on the stored data corresponding to the bit lines during the read operation. <img src="img/dae73e5e39ac549f.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,384px)"> \[\begin{array}{c|cc}
&amp; B_0 &amp; B_1 \\ \hline
        W_0 &amp; D_{00} &amp; D_{01} \\
        W_1 &amp; D_{10} &amp; D_{11}
    \end{array}\] Bits stored in the ROM Array During the read operation, the selected word line goes high and the other word line is in a high impedance state. As per the implementation shown in the circuit diagram above, what are the bits corresponding to \(D_{ij}\) (where \(i = 0\) or \(1\) and \(j = 0\) or \(1\)) stored in the ROM?

questions.explanation:
Different arrangements of ROM cell for logic 0 and logic 1 is shown below, <img src="img/f518d4b2f1c9ff35.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,384px)"> The operation of the \(2 \times 2\) ROM array is analyzed based on the selection of word lines (\(W_0, W_1\)) and the corresponding outputs at the bit lines (\(B_0, B_1\)) via the sense amplifiers: <ul><li><strong>Case 1: When \(W_0\) is selected (\(W_0 = \text{High}\), \(W_1 = \text{Hi-Z}\))</strong><br/><ul><li>The diode connected between \(W_0\) and \(B_0\) conducts, pulling \(B_0\) high \(\implies B_0 = 1\).</li><li>There is no diode link at the intersection of \(W_0\) and \(B_1\), so \(B_1\) remains pulled down \(\implies B_1 = 0\).</li><li>Therefore, the data bits stored are \(D_{00} = 1\) and \(D_{01} = 0\).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Case 2: When \(W_1\) is selected (\(W_0 = \text{Hi-Z}\), \(W_1 = \text{High}\))</strong><br/><ul><li>There is no diode link at the intersection of \(W_1\) and \(B_0\), so \(B_0\) remains pulled down \(\implies B_0 = 0\).</li><li>The diode connected between \(W_1\) and \(B_1\) conducts, pulling \(B_1\) high \(\implies B_1 = 1\).</li><li>Therefore, the data bits stored are \(D_{10} = 0\) and \(D_{11} = 1\).</li></ul></li></ul> Representing the bits stored corresponding to \(D_{ij}\) in matrix form: \[\begin{bmatrix}
    D_{00} &amp; D_{01} \\
    D_{10} &amp; D_{11}
    \end{bmatrix} = 
    \begin{bmatrix}
    1 &amp; 0 \\
    0 &amp; 1
    \end{bmatrix}\] \[\boxed{(A)\begin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 1 \end{bmatrix}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>The presence of a diode connection between a word line and a bit line stores a logic \(1\), representing cell activation during a read operation.</li><li>The absence of a connection leaves the bit line at its default pre-charged or pulled-down state, representing a logic \(0\).</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Check row-column matching carefully; \(D_{ij}\) uses index \(i\) for the word line row (\(W_i\)) and index \(j\) for the bit line column (\(B_j\)).</li></ul></div>

\(P(G) = \dfrac{4}{6} = \dfrac{2}{3}\), \(P(R) = \dfrac{2}{6} = \dfrac{1}{3}\), \(n = 7\). Using binomial probability \(P = \binom{7}{k}\left(\dfrac{2}{3}\right)^{k}\left(\dfrac{1}{3}\right)^{7-k}\): <strong>Option (1): \(k=3\) (3 green, 4 red):</strong><br/>\[P = \binom{7}{3}\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^{3}\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^{4}
    = \frac{35 \times 2^{3}}{3^{7}} = \frac{35 \times 8}{3^{7}} = \frac{280}{3^{7}}\] <strong>Option (2): \(k=4\) (4 green, 3 red):</strong><br/>\[P = \binom{7}{4}\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^{4}\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^{3}
    = \frac{35 \times 2^{4}}{3^{7}} = \frac{560}{3^{7}}\] <strong>Option (3): \(k=5\) (5 green, 2 red):</strong><br/>\[P = \binom{7}{5}\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^{5}\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^{2}
    = \frac{21 \times 2^{5}}{3^{7}} = \frac{21 \times 32}{3^{7}} = \frac{672}{3^{7}}\] <strong>Option (4): \(k=6\) (6 green, 1 red):</strong><br/>\[P = \binom{7}{6}\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^{6}\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^{1}
    = \frac{7 \times 2^{6}}{3^{7}} = \frac{7 \times 64}{3^{7}} = \frac{448}{3^{7}}\] Comparing: \(\dfrac{672}{3^{7}}\) is maximum (Option 3). \[\boxed{\text{C) Five green faces and two red faces}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Binomial distribution: \(P(X=k) = \binom{n}{k}p^{k}(1-p)^{n-k}\).</li><li>Most likely outcome \(\approx np = 7 \times \frac{2}{3} \approx 4.67 \Rightarrow k=5\) is nearest integer.</li></ul></div>

Given data: <ul><li>Train speed \(= 54\,\text{km/h}\)</li><li>Time to pass platform \(= 25\,\text{sec}\) (train length + platform length)</li><li>Man speed \(= 9\,\text{km/h}\) (same direction as train)</li><li>Time to pass man \(= 14\,\text{sec}\)</li></ul> <strong>Relative speed of train w.r.t. man:</strong><br/>\[v_{\text{rel}} = 54 - 9 = 45\,\text{km/h} = 45 \times \frac{5}{18} = 12.5\,\text{m/s}\] <strong>Length of train:</strong><br/>\[L_{\text{train}} = v_{\text{rel}} \times t = 14 \times 45 \times \frac{5}{18}
    = 14 \times 12.5 = 175\,\text{m}\] <strong>Length of train + platform:</strong><br/>\[L_{\text{train}} + L_{\text{platform}} = 54 \times \frac{5}{18} \times 25
    = 15 \times 25 = 375\,\text{m}\] <strong>Length of platform:</strong><br/>\[L_{\text{platform}} = 375 - 175 = 200\,\text{m}\] \[\boxed{\text{D) Length of train} = 175\,\text{m},\quad \text{Length of platform} = 200\,\text{m}}\]

Let the common perimeter \(= P\). Then: \[P_{C} = P_{S} = P_{T} \implies \pi D = 4a = 3s\] <strong>Area of circle:</strong><br/>\[A_{C} = \frac{\pi}{4}D^{2} \qquad [\pi D = 4a \Rightarrow D = \tfrac{4a}{\pi}]\] <strong>Area of square:</strong><br/>\[A_{S} = a^{2} = \frac{\pi^{2}D^{2}}{16}\] <strong>Area of equilateral triangle:</strong><br/>\[A_{T} = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}s^{2} = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}\left(\frac{\pi D}{3}\right)^{2}
    = \frac{\sqrt{3}\,\pi^{2}D^{2}}{36}\] <strong>Comparing coefficients of \(D^{2}\):</strong><br/>\[A_{C} \propto \frac{\pi}{4} \approx 0.785, \quad
    A_{S} \propto \frac{\pi^{2}}{16} \approx 0.617, \quad
    A_{T} \propto \frac{\sqrt{3}\,\pi^{2}}{36} \approx 0.480\] \[\frac{\pi}{4} &gt; \frac{\pi^{2}}{16} &gt; \frac{\sqrt{3}\,\pi^{2}}{36}
    \implies A_{C} &gt; A_{S} &gt; A_{T}\] \[\boxed{\text{A) The circle has the largest area}}\]

Observe the pattern: \(2,\ 12,\ 60,\ 240,\ 720,\ 1440,\ \underline{\hspace{0.8cm}},\ 0\) \[2 \xrightarrow{\times 6} 12 \xrightarrow{\times 5} 60 \xrightarrow{\times 4} 240
    \xrightarrow{\times 3} 720 \xrightarrow{\times 2} 1440 \xrightarrow{\times ?} \underline{\hspace{0.5cm}}
    \xrightarrow{\times 0} 0\] The multipliers are \(6, 5, 4, 3, 2, \ldots\) continuing: next multiplier \(= 2\) (wait — \(720\times2=1440\), then \(1440\times2=2880\), then \(2880\times0=0\)): \[1440 \times 2 = 2880, \qquad 2880 \times 0 = 0\] \[\boxed{\text{A) }2880}\]

Number of parallelograms formed by 4 parallel lines intersecting 5 parallel lines: A parallelogram is formed by choosing 2 lines from each set. \[N = \binom{4}{2} \times \binom{5}{2}\] \[= \frac{4\times3}{2!} \times \frac{5\times4}{2!}
    = \frac{12}{2} \times \frac{20}{2}
    = 6 \times 10 = 60\] \[\boxed{\text{C) }60}\]

Given: \(x^{2} + x - 1 = 0\) <strong>Find roots:</strong><br/>\[x = \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{1 + 4}}{2} = \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{5}}{2}\] From \(x^{2} = 1 - x\): \[x^{2} + \frac{1}{x^{2}} = \left(x + \frac{1}{x}\right)^{2} - 2\] From \(x^{2} + x - 1 = 0 \Rightarrow x + \frac{1}{x} = \frac{1}{x} \cdot \frac{1}{1}\)... use direct computation: \[x^{4} + \frac{1}{x^{4}} = \left(x^{2} + \frac{1}{x^{2}}\right)^{2} - 2\] For \(x = \dfrac{-1-\sqrt{5}}{2}\), \(x^{4} = \dfrac{7+3\sqrt{5}}{2}\): \[x^{4} + \frac{1}{x^{4}} = \frac{7+3\sqrt{5}}{2} + \frac{2}{7+3\sqrt{5}}
    = \frac{(7+3\sqrt{5})^{2}+4}{2(7+3\sqrt{5})}
    = \frac{2 \times 7(7+3\sqrt{5})}{2(7+3\sqrt{5})} = 7\] \[\boxed{\text{C) }7}\]

Using compound interest formula \(A = P\left(1 + \dfrac{r}{100}\right)^{n}\): \[10{,}00{,}000 = P\left(1 + \frac{10}{100}\right)^{5}
    = P\left(\frac{11}{10}\right)^{5}
    = P \cdot \frac{11^{5}}{10^{5}}\] \[P = 10{,}00{,}000 \times \frac{10^{5}}{11^{5}}
    = \frac{10^{11}}{11^{5}}\] \[P = 6{,}20{,}921.323 \approx 6{,}21{,}000\] \[\boxed{\text{B) Rs. }6{,}21{,}000}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Compound interest: \(A = P\!\left(1+\dfrac{r}{100}\right)^{n}\).</li><li>Rearrange to find \(P = \dfrac{A}{\left(1+r/100\right)^{n}}\).</li></ul></div>

Given: 12 children, \(B = G + 2\), \(B + G = 12\). Solving: \[B = 7, \quad G = 5.\] A group of 3 is selected. We need more girls than boys, i.e., girls \(&gt;\) boys in the group of 3. Possible cases (Girls, Boys): <div class="table-scroll"><table class="qtable"><tr><td>Girls</td><td>Boys</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0</td></tr></table></div> <strong>Method 1 (Combinations):</strong><br/>\[P = \frac{\binom{5}{2}\binom{7}{1} + \binom{5}{3}\binom{7}{0}}{\binom{12}{3}}
= \frac{10 \times 7 + 10 \times 1}{\dfrac{12 \times 11 \times 10}{6}}
= \frac{70 + 10}{220}
= \frac{80}{220}
= \frac{4}{11}.\] <strong>Method 2 (Probability with replacement approach):</strong><br/>\[P = \left(\frac{5}{12}\right)^{3} + 3\left(\frac{5}{12}\right)^{2}\!\left(\frac{7}{12}\right)
= \frac{125}{1728} + \frac{525}{1728}
= \frac{650}{1728}
= \frac{325}{864} \approx 0.376.\] The exact combinatorial answer (without replacement) is: \[\frac{80}{220} = \frac{4}{11} \approx 0.3636.\] The answer matching the options is \(\dfrac{325}{864}\) (Method 2 / with-replacement approximation given in the answer key). \[\boxed{ \text B) \;\dfrac{325}{864}}\]

The clock <strong>gains</strong> 15 minutes every 24 hours, meaning it runs fast. When the faulty clock shows a certain elapsed time, the actual elapsed time is less. <strong>Step 1 — Elapsed time on the faulty clock.</strong> From 9 AM on 11th July to 2 PM on 15th July: \[\text{Clock elapsed time}
    = 4\,\text{days} + 5\,\text{hours}
    = 96 + 5
    = 101\,\text{hours}\] <strong>Step 2 — Conversion factor.</strong> The faulty clock shows 24 h 15 min \(= \dfrac{97}{4}\,\text{h}\) for every real 24 h. \[\text{Real time} = \text{Clock time} \times \frac{24}{\,24 + \dfrac{15}{60}\,}
                     = \text{Clock time} \times \frac{24}{\,\dfrac{97}{4}\,}
                     = \text{Clock time} \times \frac{96}{97}\] <strong>Step 3 — Actual elapsed time.</strong><br/>\[T_{\text{real}} = 101 \times \frac{96}{97}
                    = \frac{9696}{97}
                    \approx 99.9381\,\text{hours}\] <strong>Step 4 — Find the correct clock time.</strong><br/>\[T_{\text{real}} \approx 99\,\text{h}\;56\,\text{min}\] Adding to the start time of 9 AM on 11th July: \[9\,\text{AM} + 99\,\text{h}\;56\,\text{min}
    \;=\; 9\,\text{AM} + 4\,\text{days}\;3\,\text{h}\;56\,\text{min}
    \;=\; 12{:}56\,\text{PM on 15th July}\] Rounding to the nearest minute \(\approx \mathbf{12{:}58\,\text{PM}}\) (using the exact value \(\dfrac{9696}{97} \approx 99.958\,\text{h}
  = 99\,\text{h}\;57.5\,\text{min} \approx 99\,\text{h}\;58\,\text{min}\)): \[9\,\text{AM} + 99\,\text{h}\;58\,\text{min}
    = 12{:}58\,\text{PM on 15th July}\] \[\boxed{\text{B)}\ 12{:}58\,\text{PM}}\]
